{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2021",
    "module": "commonjs",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "outDir": "./dist"
  }
}

// Your tsconfig.json is actually excellent for TypeScript development. Here's why:

// ✅ TypeScript-Specific Features:

// strict: true - Enables TypeScript's strongest type checking

// forceConsistentCasingInFileNames: true - TypeScript compiler feature

// skipLibCheck: true - Skips checking .d.ts files (TypeScript declarations)

// outDir: "./dist" - Compiles .ts files to .js in dist folder

// ✅ What It Does:

// Takes your TypeScript code (.ts files)

// Type-checks it with strict rules

// Compiles it to JavaScript (ES2021) in the dist folder

// Uses CommonJS modules (perfect for Node.js)

